Step 1: Find thetotal impedance \((Z)\) in the circuit.
The angular frequency \((ω)\) is given by;
\(\Rightarrow ω=2πf\)
Substituting the values we get;
\(\Rightarrow ω=2×π×750=4712.39~\text{rad/s}\)
The inductive reactance \((X_L)\) is given by;
\(X_L=ωL=4712.39×0.1803≈850.74~Ω\)
The capacitive reactance \((X_C)\) is given by;
\(X_C=\frac{1}{ωC}=\frac{1}{4712.39×10×10^{−6}}≈21.24~Ω\)
The total impedance \((Z)\) in the circuit is given by:
\(Z=\sqrt{R^2+(X_L−X_C)^2}\)
Substituting the values we get;
\(Z=\sqrt{100^2+(850.74−21.24)^2}≈838.5~Ω\)

Step 2:Find the time in which the resistance will get heatedby \(10^\circ \text
{C}\)
The RMS current \((I_{RMS})\) can be calculated using Ohm's law;
\(I_{RMS}=\frac{V_{RMS}}{Z}=\frac{20}{838.5}≈0.0239~\text A\)
The power \((P)\) dissipated in the resistor is given by;
\(P=I^2_{RMS}×R=(0.0239)2×100≈0.057~\text W\)
The heat \((Q)\) required to raise the temperature of the resistor by \(Δθ\) is
given by;
\(\Rightarrow Q=S×Δθ=2×10=20~\text J\)
\(P=\frac{Q}{T}⟹T=\frac{Q}{P}\)
Substituting the values we get;
\(\Rightarrow T=\frac{20}{0.057}≈350.88~\text s\)
